{"adjudications":[{"id":"F1","decision":"rejected","reason":"The King's Cross feint survives and still works. Tuppence names its purpose: 'if they think we're going to Sir James, this will put them off the scent.' Pursuit is still made visible by 'some one behind us', by the drunk who reaches the steps, and by Sir James's 'You have been followed here.' Losing the two watcher sentences weakens the build-up of surveillance, but that is a legitimate compression, not a broken setup.","evidence":[{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":26,"quote":"but I feel as though there was some one behind us."},{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":29,"quote":"The man who had stopped them reached the foot of the steps."},{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":72,"quote":"You have been followed here--not a doubt of it."}]},{"id":"F2","decision":"rejected","reason":"This is an artistic tradeoff rather than damage. Tuppence's pert energy survives in 'I'm really rather clever!' and in the butting, and Sir James's 'Checkmate to the Young Adventurers' in chapter 027 still works without this boast. The cut is not in the essential plan.","evidence":[{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":23,"quote":"I can’t help thinking that I’m really rather clever!"},{"document":"027.revision","paragraph":39,"quote":"“Checkmate to the Young Adventurers,” he said"}]},{"id":"F3","decision":"confirmed","reason":"Partly confirmed. In the draft, Jane's 'doctor' remark now hangs with no reaction, so it no longer shows that her bluff has landed. The source's relief beat gives the line its point. The quiet man's menace is already carried by V4 and the wrist-twisting, so the 'seen right through me' sentence need not be restored.","evidence":[{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":52,"quote":"I murmured something about the ‘doctor’ having hurt my wrist."},{"document":"026.source","paragraph":79,"quote":"looked relieved when I said that."}]},{"id":"F4","decision":"uncertain","reason":"The captivity sequence still reads coherently. Jane discovers the false nurse, half-hypnotises herself into apathy, and is later moved to Soho, which implies she survived the tests. Sir James's comment was marked optional. Restoring the outcome clause would help, but it is not clearly necessary within the word budget.","evidence":[{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":62,"quote":"I think I almost hypnotized myself."},{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":64,"quote":"One night I was whisked off to London, back to the house in Soho."}]},{"id":"F5","decision":"rejected","reason":"The source itself pairs 'At last I do' with 'morally certain… for some time', so the two can be read as a gradation, not a contradiction. The essential beat survives: Tuppence hesitates and asks him to speak. The brandy logic and 'logic of facts' stay attributed to Sir James, and his claims remain unendorsed by the narrator.","evidence":[{"document":"026.revision","paragraph":76,"quote":"“Yes, please. _You_ know who Mr. Brown is, don’t you?”"},{"document":"026.source","paragraph":115,"quote":"I have been morally certain of\nhis identity for some time"}]}],"replacements":[{"old":"I murmured something about the ‘doctor’ having hurt my wrist.","new":"I murmured something about the ‘doctor’ having hurt my wrist. She looked relieved when I said that.","reason":"Restore the source reaction so the remark signals that the bluff succeeded (source par. 79).","finding_ids":["F3"]},{"old":"She shuddered. In a minute she resumed:","new":"She shuddered.","reason":"Optional trim to offset the restored words; the shudder beat is kept.","finding_ids":[]},{"old":"watching me like a lynx all the time","new":"watching me like a lynx","reason":"Optional trim to meet the word target; the idiom is preserved.","finding_ids":[]},{"old":"In the end I got up and walked round","new":"I got up and walked round","reason":"Optional trim to meet the word target; her caution is still conveyed by the following sentences.","finding_ids":[]}],"remaining_concerns":["F4: the outcome 'I managed once again to hold my own with them' is still absent. The nurse's imitation tests end without stating that Jane passed them; it is implied only by what follows.","The net change is estimated at -4 words rather than -3; code measurement should confirm the result."]}
